#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114966 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae016162-6fee-5bb7-950f-fb7aa1ce4e11" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits of Exponential Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "f0f39f69-9d92-56bb-90ac-b8dc7ba8cff9" :question/math-academy-id "e-4254" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} -3\\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\nlim_(x → - ∞) - 3(\\frac{1}{2})^{x} =-3 \\cdot lim_(x → - ∞)(\\frac{1}{2})^{x}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1717/Source/Images/5846ea3956bb36f7b5955be3d8294c64.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function increases without bound as $x$ decreases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x = \\infty\n$$\n\nHowever, the negative factor $-3$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot \\lim_{x\\to-\\infty} \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^x = -\\infty\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114978 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58de617-785d-5318-9d12-99651e030eb2" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Using Direct Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "b0ab103e-1521-507e-b69b-5fd5256cf051" :question/math-academy-id "e-45" :question/problem "Find $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 1} (3^{2x} + 2^{3x} + 1)$." :question/worked-solution "We can evaluate this limit using direct substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → 1)(3^{2x} + 2^{3x} + 1) &= 3^{2(1)} + 2^{3(1)} + 1 \\\\\n&= 9 + 8 + 1 \\\\\n&= 18\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114986 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6835b52b-adaa-5c66-9f6d-d32591864659" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits Using the Laws of Exponents and the Algebra of Limits"}] :question/id #uuid "bfe24c48-c5eb-59ca-b34d-7e0540079c44" :question/math-academy-id "e-46" :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} -5 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{1+x}$." :question/worked-solution "We can use the laws of exponents and the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → ∞) - 5(\\frac{1}{3})^{1 + x} &= -5lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{1}{3})^{1 + x} \\\\\n&=-5lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{1}{3})^{1} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3})^{x} \\\\\n&=-5lim_(x → ∞)\\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3})^{x} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{5}{3} \\cdot lim_(x → ∞)(\\frac{1}{3})^{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\left(\\dfrac 1 3\\right)^x\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1717/Source/Images/5a86ab6087c88635878b30b98ffed525.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. So\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{x} = 0\n$$\n\nand finally, we have\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{5}{3}\\cdot \\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{x} = -\\dfrac 5 3\\cdot 0 = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6835b52b-adaa-5c66-9f6d-d32591864659" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Infinite Limits Using the Laws of Exponents and the Algebra of Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a5a690f-c295-543c-8564-9e503a928a72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114124} {:answer/id #uuid "b7827b3e-6eef-5cca-88b2-6f6ea09c9c2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "DNE" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114125} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5c4e0c-59be-590d-bfd7-dd5edf663f4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114126} {:answer/id #uuid "e0305a24-11ff-5c0e-9933-0992e02c6f78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∞" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114127} {:answer/id #uuid "45e43b6c-cc20-5f3a-be9d-e26a9e3ecaf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ∞" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd5c4e0c-59be-590d-bfd7-dd5edf663f4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114126} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0913fff-cbea-5315-8c93-5a1de2156fe6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186114123}] :question/id #uuid "8749a00a-586e-55ad-83f3-b90c135bdf18" :question/math-academy-id "q-47081" :question/problem "What is $lim_(x → ∞)e^{(-3x + 1)}$?"}]}
